{"id":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#prop:volume-form-uniqueness","title":"prop:volume-form-uniqueness","path":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2.md","line":1368,"body":"","kind":"theorem","status":"not_started","tex":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2.tex#prop:volume-form-uniqueness","uses":["[[references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#def:volume-form-pointwise]]","[[references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#prop:volume-form-value-one]]"],"inspired_by":[],"bindings":[],"is_index":false,"body_html":"","formal":[],"tex_content":{"path":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2.tex","label":"prop:volume-form-uniqueness","line":2322,"source":"\\begin{proposition}[Uniqueness of $dV_g$]\n\\label{prop:volume-form-uniqueness}\n\n\\uses{def:volume-form-pointwise, prop:volume-form-value-one}\n\n\n\n\nIf $\\theta$ is an alternating $n$-form on $T_xM$ taking value $1$ on a positively oriented orthonormal basis $(E_i|_x)$, then $\\theta=dV_g|_x$.\n\\end{proposition}\n\n\\begin{proof}\n\n\\uses{prop:volume-form-value-one}\n\\sketch Every top-degree form is determined by its value on one basis. Both $\\theta$ and $dV_g|_x$ equal the determinant form of $(E_i|_x)$ because both take value $1$ there.\n\\end{proof}","html":" <p></p><section class=\"theorem\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Proposition</div></section>[Uniqueness of <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$dV_g$\">$dV_g$</span>] <span class=\"tex-label\" id=\"tex-label-70726f703a766f6c756d652d666f726d2d756e697175656e657373\"></span> <p></p><a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FLeeRiemannian%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2Fchapter2%23def%3Avolume-form-pointwise\">↗ def:volume-form-pointwise</a> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FLeeRiemannian%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2Fchapter2%23prop%3Avolume-form-value-one\">↗ prop:volume-form-value-one</a> <p></p>If <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\theta $\">$\\theta $</span> is an alternating <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$n$\">$n$</span>-form on <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$T_xM$\">$T_xM$</span> taking value <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$1$\">$1$</span> on a positively oriented orthonormal basis <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$(E_i|_x)$\">$(E_i|_x)$</span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\theta =dV_g|_x$\">$\\theta =dV_g|_x$</span>.  <p></p><section class=\"theorem proof\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Proof</div><p><a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FLeeRiemannian%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2Fchapter2%23prop%3Avolume-form-value-one\">↗ prop:volume-form-value-one</a> \\sketch Every top-degree form is determined by its value on one basis. Both <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\theta $\">$\\theta $</span> and <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$dV_g|_x$\">$dV_g|_x$</span> equal the determinant form of <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$(E_i|_x)$\">$(E_i|_x)$</span> because both take value <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$1$\">$1$</span> there. </p><span class=\"qed\" aria-label=\"证毕\" style=\"display:block;text-align:right\">□</span></section>","warnings":["部分 TeX 排版细节未完整还原（已保留可读内容）: sketch"]},"previous":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#prop:volume-form-value-one","next":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#lem:top-forms-proportional","relations":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#def:volume-form-pointwise","title":"def:volume-form-pointwise","label":"def:volume-form-pointwise"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#prop:volume-form-value-one","title":"prop:volume-form-value-one","labe